1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
A @RestController that returns objects is
not yet a REST API.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:14,719
REST is a contract style: resources,
verbs, representations, and status codes
that clients can trust — especially when
the resource is a parcel moving through a
harbor.

3
00:00:14,839 --> 00:00:24,920
Teams often start with "JSON endpoints"
and discover the pain later. One method
returns 200 with an error string in the
body. Another returns 200 for create.

4
00:00:25,039 --> 00:00:32,320
Paths look like /getParcel and
/createParcel — RPC painted with HTTP.
Content types drift.

5
00:00:32,439 --> 00:00:42,039
Trucking clients cannot cache, cannot tell
success from failure without parsing
bodies, and cannot evolve without breaking
every scanner. Spring does not force good
REST on you.

6
00:00:42,159 --> 00:01:00,280
It gives you the tools to express a
deliberate contract. Think in resources,
not procedure names. A parcel is a
resource at /parcels/{id}. A collection
lives at /parcels. Creating a parcel is
POST /parcels with a body.

7
00:01:00,399 --> 00:01:13,159
Updating is PUT or PATCH depending on your
semantics. Deleting is DELETE. Reading is
GET. Tracking events hang under the
parcel: /parcels/{id}/events.

8
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:24,400
The Java method names can be create or
get; the public contract is the URL plus
the verb. Look at this java for a moment.
ResponseEntity is deliberate response
control.

9
00:01:24,519 --> 00:01:38,719
201 Created with a Location header tells
clients where the new parcel lives. 404
for an unknown id is a status, not a soft
null body with 200. GET stays safe and
idempotent; POST creates.

10
00:01:38,839 --> 00:01:51,839
Media types matter too: declare produces
and consumes when you support more than
one representation, and fail loudly on
mismatch instead of guessing. Versioning
and representation design deserve a short
honest take.

11
00:01:51,959 --> 00:02:06,199
Prefer stable resource names and evolve
fields carefully. If you must version,
pick a strategy and stick to it — URI
prefix like /v1/parcels or a version media
type — but do not invent three strategies
in one codebase.

12
00:02:06,319 --> 00:02:19,719
Separate request DTOs from response DTOs
when the shapes diverge; exposing a JPA
entity as the API body couples persistence
to every trucking client. Hypermedia is
optional; honesty is not.

13
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:33,240
You do not need a full HATEOAS graph on
day one. You do need documented fields,
predictable nullability, and stable enum
strings for parcel states. Prefer ISO-8601
timestamps.

14
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:41,240
When a collection of events is empty,
return 200 with an empty list, not 404 —
404 means the parcel resource itself is
missing.

15
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:48,360
A frequent wrong turn is treating every
successful Java return as HTTP 200,
including creates and deletes.

16
00:02:48,480 --> 00:03:00,440
Another is encoding actions in the path
(/parcels/{id}/markDelivered) when a state
transition could be a PATCH on the parcel
or a POST to a subordinate resource.

17
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:10,960
A third is leaking internal database ids
in URLs you are not willing to support
forever — prefer opaque public tracking
ids. Controllers can now claim paths.

18
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:22,080
Claiming a path still leaves a hole: what
happens when the JSON is well-formed yet
the customs fields are missing, blank, or
nonsense — and who should reject that
before the tracking service runs?

19
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:24,020
That boundary is validation.
